steps.body:
A function is a value, so you can store one in a `const`. A **function expression** is the `function` keyword used as a value, usually with no name after it:

```javascript
const add = function (a, b) {
  return a + b;
};
```

An **arrow function** is the shorter form you will see everywhere. When the body is one expression, drop the braces and `return`: the value is returned automatically (an implicit return).

```javascript
const double = (x) => x * 2;
const shout = (text) => {
  const loud = text.toUpperCase();
  return `${loud}!`;
};
```

Once you add braces you have a block body, and then you must write `return` yourself. Forget it and the arrow returns `undefined`. JavaScript lets you drop the parentheses around a single parameter, but this course always keeps them.

**The gotcha:** only `function` declarations can be called before their line. A function stored in a `const` cannot be used until that line has run, so calling it earlier throws a `ReferenceError`. Define arrows before the code that calls them.

Arrows also do not get their own `this`. That matters in the classes lessons, so just remember it for now.

example.code:
const double = (x) => x * 2;
const add = (a, b) => a + b;
const shout = (text) => {
  const loud = text.toUpperCase();
  return `${loud}!`;
};

console.log(`double(21) = ${double(21)}`);
console.log(`add(2, 3) = ${add(2, 3)}`);
console.log(`shout("hey") = ${shout("hey")}`);

