practice.solution:
const addTen = (x) => x + 10;
const isBig = (x) => x > 5;

const applyN = (fn, x, n) => {
  let result = x;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    result = fn(result);
  }
  return result;
};

const keepIf = (arr, test) => {
  const kept = [];
  for (const item of arr) {
    if (test(item)) {
      kept.push(item);
    }
  }
  return kept;
};

console.log(`applyN(addTen, 0, 3) = ${applyN(addTen, 0, 3)}`);
console.log(`keepIf: ${keepIf([3, 8, 12, 5], isBig).join(", ")}`);

steps.body:
`arr.forEach(callback)` calls your callback once for each element, in order. The callback receives the value, the index, and the whole array. Declare only the parameters you need.

```javascript
const names = ["Ana", "Ben"];
names.forEach((name, index, list) => {
  console.log(`${index + 1} of ${list.length}: ${name}`);
});
```

Use `forEach` for side effects: printing, or updating a `let` declared outside the loop. It always returns `undefined`, so `const x = arr.forEach(...)` is a bug.

**The gotcha:** `forEach` cannot stop early. `break` is a SyntaxError inside the callback, and `return` only ends the call for the current item, so the loop goes on with the next one. When you need to stop at the first match, use `for...of` with `break`:

```javascript harness
const names = ["Ana", "Ben"];
{{code}}
```

```javascript
for (const [i, name] of names.entries()) {
  if (name === "Ben") {
    console.log(`Found at ${i}`);
    break;
  }
}
```

example.code:
const groceries = ["Milk", "Eggs", "Bread", "Apples"];
const prices = [2.5, 3.25, 4, 1.75];

groceries.forEach((item, index) => {
  console.log(`${index + 1}. ${item}`);
});

let total = 0;
prices.forEach((price) => {
  total += price;
});
console.log(`Total: ${total.toFixed(2)}`);

for (const [i, price] of prices.entries()) {
  if (price > 3) {
    console.log(`First over 3: ${groceries[i]}`);
    break;
  }
}

